op7t-0196: the speakers from boot, in the kernel (bsd/dev/sweet_sound.c)

2026-11-11: iOS's own sound played through the 7T's speakers with the chain
run from userland (tools/sweetaudio.py, sweet-media.sh). The user wants it
to work straight from the image, at once, without scripts. This does in the
kernel what those did:

- /dev/redwood-audio made at boot (sweet_smmu.c's 0195 node, now
  sweet_audio_dev_make()), so the RedwoodAudio HAL plug-in opens it the first
  time the audio server loads it: no mediaserverd restart;
- a thread, after sweetsounddelay s (15): the audio DSP started
  (sweet_adsp_start, stage 6 awaited); SE4 as an I2C master (GCC votes, S4 at
  XO, pins 51/52); both TFA9874s set up (I2C reset, keys, NXP's rev 0x0c74
  values, the 7T's tfa98xx.cnt device + "speaker" writes built in) and muted;
  MI2S pins 137/138/140; bit clock 3.072 MHz and AFE port 0x1006 (I2S SD1,
  32-bit, 48 kHz) started; amplifiers out of powerdown on that clock;
- the stream: the 0194 buffer mapped, ADM path (16-bit, no processing), ASM
  session 1 opened/routed/run; every period the DSP gives back refilled from
  the ring (sweet_audio_ring_take), sweetsoundgain dB below full (default
  12; no speaker protection), clipped; amplifiers on at the first sound, off
  after 3 s of silence.

sweetsound=0 turns it off (the userland tools still work then). kern.sweetsound:
stage (7 = playing; negative = stopped there) and counters. Log "HOTDOGB SOUND:".
Syntax-checked with clang -Wall -Wextra on stub headers.

Apply after 0195.
